Project Proposal: Simulating Decoherence of
Non-Classical Quantum States via the Monte Carlo
Wavefunction
Numerical Physics — Anna Iranzo & Andriana Misailidi
October 20, 2026
1 Introduction & Motivation
Understanding environmental decoherence is fundamental to open quantum dynamics and quantum
information. While the standard Lindblad master equation evolves an N × N density matrix ρ(t)
with O(N2) memory scaling, the Monte Carlo Wavefunction (MCWF) method evolves an
ensemble of N-dimensional pure state vectors |ψ(t)⟩ undergoing deterministic non-unitary evolution
interspersed with stochastic quantum jumps (O(N) scaling).
In this project, we propose to model the decoherence dynamics of macroscopic superpositions
(Schr¨odinger cat states) subject to dissipative (photon loss) and dephasing environments.
2 Physical Model & Numerical Formulation
We consider a single bosonic cavity mode in a truncated Fock space (Ncut ≈ 40–60):
• Schr¨odinger Cat State: |ψcat⟩ ∝ |α⟩ ± | − α⟩, parameterized by coherent amplitude α.
